  1. Why use the Excess Test Statistics method

  To evaluate the absolute level of publication bias: Previous methods may only give relative results, such as relative to randomized controlled trials (RCTs). 
The Excess Test Statistics method can study the absolute level of publication bias by comparing the observed test statistic distribution with the counterfactual distribution, 
and more comprehensively understand the problems in the research field.

  To consider the differences between different research methods: Different research methods (such as double difference method DID, instrumental variable method IV, 
regression discontinuity method RDD, etc.) may have different treatment methods and influencing factors in the research process. This method flexibly calibrates different 
counterfactual t distributions for each method, and endogenizes the potential differences between methods that affect the shape, location and scale of the distribution, so as 
to more accurately evaluate the publication bias and p-hacking situation under each method.


  2.Principles of Excess Test Statistics

  A.Construction of counterfactual distribution

  -Assumptions: Assume that in the region of z > 5, the distribution of the test statistic is not affected by p-hacking or publication bias, because in this range 
far away from the traditional significance threshold, the motivation for p-hacking can be considered zero.
  -Distribution calibration: Calibrate a non-central t distribution for each research method, and adjust the degrees of freedom (df) and non-centrality parameter (np)
to make the distribution fit the observed distribution as much as possible in the region of (z > 5). Specifically, for degrees of freedom from (0) to (10), calculate 
the non-centrality parameter that minimizes the quality difference between the observed distribution and the expected distribution in the region of (z > 5), and then select
the "best" distribution from these (10) optimized t distributions. The degrees of freedom parameter is a positive integer, and the non-centrality parameter is a positive real
number, which is optimized with steps of (1) and (0.01) respectively.

  B.Calculation of Excess Test Statistics
  For a specific region, the difference between the observed cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the t-statistic is calculated, and then the difference between the
corresponding CDF calculated according to the calibrated t-distribution is subtracted to obtain the excess test statistic for the region, that is, the difference between 
the observed quality and the expected quality.

  3. Analysis of test result

  A.Overall fitting effect
  As can be seen from Figure 4 and Table A24 in the online appendix, the calibrated t-distribution matches the observed distribution very well in the (5 < z < infinity) region,
and the maximum difference in quality between the two does not exceed 0.001, which indicates that the calibration of the counterfactual distribution is successful.

  B.Analysis of results in different z value intervals
  (0 < z < 1.65) region: Except for the IV method, the quality difference between the observed values ​​and the expected values ​​of other methods is small. The IV method has a total
quality loss of 6.3% in this region. Relative to the expected IV quality, about 16% of the insignificant IV test statistics are missing, which may suggest that the IV 
method selectively excludes insignificant results in this insignificant region.
  ([1.65 < z < 1.96) region: The quality difference between the observed values ​​and the expected values ​​of all methods is small, and only the IV method has excess quality, 
indicating that the IV method may have some p-hacking behavior in this region close to the traditional significance threshold.
  ([1.96 < z < 2.58) region: This is the region with the most significant difference in results. The IV method has a total mass excess of 4.1%, that is, 30% more
statistically significant test statistics than expected, and its excess size is more than 5 times that of DID and RCT. DID and RCT also have a certain degree of distortion, 
with 0.8% mass excess respectively. The RDD method performs better, and the statistically significant test statistics in this area are always less than expected, indicating
that the RDD method is relatively less affected by p-hacking and publication bias.
  ([2.58 < z < 5) region: The IV method has a total mass excess of 1.9%, that is, 7.8% more statistically significant test statistics than expected. The statistically
significant test statistics of the DID, RDD and RCT methods in this area are all less than expected, less than 3.7%, 10.4% and 15.3%, respectively.


  *Comparison with RCT
  In the online appendix Table A23, the observed RCT distribution is used instead of the calibrated t distribution for comparison. The results show that in the non-significant area, 
the missing amount of non-significant test statistics of DID and IV is more than twice that of RDD; in the just-significant area, the difference between the quasi-experimental method 
and RCT distribution is small; in the two-star significant area, IV estimates that 5.4\% of the results are misassigned, which is twice that of DID and 11 times that of RDD. However,
this simple method has problems in the [2.58 < z < 5) interval because it implicitly assumes that the effect sizes of different studies are homogeneous, so the article prefers to use 
the method of calibrating the input distribution.

